course: dictionary
lesson: 16081
title: Wan: pale, tired and weak
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Wan means pale, and looking ill or tired. «Mia looked wan after a week in bed with flu.» The clue is flu: she was poorly, so wan describes how she looked, not a happy colour.
explain: A wan smile is weak, tired or sad, not a big grin. «Her smile was wan, and she did not laugh.» The words did not laugh tell you the smile was small and sad.
explain: Old poems use wan for faint, weak light. Check your meaning by putting «pale» in the sentence instead. If it still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Wan means pale and looking ill or tired.
fact: «A wan face» is a pale face that looks unwell.
fact: «A wan smile» is a weak, tired or sad smile.
fact: In old poems, «wan light» means faint, weak light.
fact: Wan is an adjective, so it describes a noun, and its forms are wan, wanner and wannest.
exercise: «Mia looked wan after a week in bed with flu.» What does wan mean? | *Pale and tired | Very hungry | Bright and cheerful | Very loud
exercise: «Her smile was wan, and she did not laugh.» What kind of smile is this? | A loud, cheeky laugh | A quick wink | *A weak, tired or sad smile | A big, happy grin
exercise: «The wan light of the lamp flickered in the old hall.» Find and copy the word that describes the light as faint. | hall | old | lamp | *wan
test: Sam says, «You look wan, shall we go home?» What does Sam mean? | You look loud | You look lost | *You look pale and tired | You look very happy
test: Which smile is a wan smile? | A loud burst of laughter | *A weak, tired smile | A wide, cheeky grin | A quick wink
exam: Jayden says, «Her face is wan, so she must be happy.» Is Jayden right? | No, wan means very loud | Yes, wan means rosy and happy | Yes, wan means bright and cheerful | *No, wan means pale and tired
